Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables / columns and their potential usage  

customer_transactions  
• customer_id : needed to aggregate balances per user  
• txn_date : supplies the transaction time‑stamp – will be converted to month (YYYY‑MM) so that balances “as at the 1st of each month” can be derived  
• txn_type : lets us understand whether positive amounts are deposits or withdrawals (may be helpful when interpreting negative balances)  
• txn_amount : signed amount that builds each customer’s running balance  

(Other tables do not hold monetary transactions or balances, so they are not needed.)

Typical conditions that will be useful later  
• DATE(txn_date) < first‑day‑of‑current‑month            → to obtain cumulative balance up to the last day of the previous month  
• strftime('%Y‑%m', txn_date)                            → to extract YYYY‑MM text for grouping  
• HAVING month_no <> first_month_no                      → to skip the very first month in the final output  
• CASE WHEN balance < 0 THEN 0 END                       → to zero‑out negative customer balances before summing

Exploratory SQL queries  
(The 10 queries below move from simple inspection toward the logic required for the final solution.  
Each returns no more than 20 rows.)

```sql
-- 1. Earliest and latest transaction dates available
SELECT MIN(txn_date) AS min_date,
       MAX(txn_date) AS max_date
FROM customer_transactions;
```

```sql
-- 2. List distinct transaction types that exist
SELECT DISTINCT txn_type
FROM customer_transactions
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Preview 20 raw customer transactions
SELECT *
FROM customer_transactions
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Get the first 20 transactions ordered by customer and date
SELECT customer_id,
       txn_date,
       txn_type,
       txn_amount
FROM customer_transactions
ORDER BY customer_id,
         DATE(txn_date)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Distinct year‑month pairs present in the data (ascending)
SELECT DISTINCT strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS month
FROM customer_transactions
ORDER BY month
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Monthly total of all transaction amounts (raw, not cumulative)
SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date)   AS month,
       SUM(txn_amount)              AS month_net_amount
FROM customer_transactions
GROUP BY month
ORDER BY month
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Running balance per customer at the end of each month
SELECT customer_id,
       strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date)            AS month,
       SUM(txn_amount)                        AS month_end_balance
FROM customer_transactions
GROUP BY customer_id,
         month
ORDER BY customer_id,
         month
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Cumulative balance for every customer up to (but not including) the first day of each month
--    (this is the balance we will later treat as "as‑of the 1st")
WITH months AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT DATE(strftime('%Y-%m-01', txn_date)) AS first_day
  FROM customer_transactions
)
SELECT m.first_day                                                  AS month_start,
       ct.customer_id,
       SUM(ct.txn_amount)                                           AS balance_to_date
FROM months AS m
JOIN customer_transactions AS ct
  ON DATE(ct.txn_date) < m.first_day
GROUP BY m.first_day,
         ct.customer_id
ORDER BY m.first_day,
         ct.customer_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Apply the rule “negative balances become zero” and get the per‑month total
WITH months AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT DATE(strftime('%Y-%m-01', txn_date)) AS first_day
  FROM customer_transactions
),
balances AS (
  SELECT m.first_day      AS month_start,
         ct.customer_id,
         SUM(ct.txn_amount) AS raw_bal
  FROM months AS m
  JOIN customer_transactions AS ct
    ON DATE(ct.txn_date) < m.first_day
  GROUP BY m.first_day,
           ct.customer_id
)
SELECT month_start,
       SUM(CASE WHEN raw_bal < 0 THEN 0 ELSE raw_bal END) AS total_balance
FROM balances
GROUP BY month_start
ORDER BY month_start
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Same as 9 but ignore the very first month (acts only as baseline)
WITH months AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT DATE(strftime('%Y-%m-01', txn_date)) AS first_day
  FROM customer_transactions
),
ordered_months AS (
  SELECT first_day,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY first_day) AS rn
  FROM months
),
balances AS (
  SELECT om.first_day           AS month_start,
         ct.customer_id,
         SUM(ct.txn_amount)     AS raw_bal
  FROM ordered_months AS om
  JOIN customer_transactions AS ct
    ON DATE(ct.txn_date) < om.first_day
  GROUP BY om.first_day,
           ct.customer_id
)
SELECT month_start,
       SUM(CASE WHEN raw_bal < 0 THEN 0 ELSE raw_bal END) AS total_balance_prev_month
FROM balances
WHERE month_start NOT IN (SELECT MIN(first_day) FROM ordered_months)   -- drop first month
GROUP BY month_start
ORDER BY month_start
LIMIT 20;
```

The progression above inspects the data and progressively constructs the logic needed to answer:  
“For each month (except the first), show the total of all users’ prior‑month balances, treating any negative customer balance as zero.”
